.TH SH 1
.SH NAME
sh \- the SIEOS shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.RB [ \-l ]
.br
.B sh \-c
.I line
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sh
reads lines from its terminal and runs them, one at a time. A line is split
into words at spaces and tabs; a part between double quotes keeps its
spaces, and the quotes go. A word that starts with
.B #
starts a comment: it and the rest of the line are ignored. At most 31 words
are kept.
.PP
If the first word names a built-in command (below), the shell runs it
itself. Any other word is a program:
.BI /bin/ name\fR,
or the file itself when the word holds a
.BR / .
The program is started with the words as its arguments (at most 255
characters in all) and the shell waits for it to end. A last word
.B &
starts it without waiting: the shell prints its process number in
brackets, and before a later prompt says how it ended
.RB ( "[pid] done, status" " N, or what signal ended it)."
.SS Jobs and Ctrl-C
At the prompt each program starts in a process group of its own, which
its children join, and the terminal is told that group runs in the
foreground. Ctrl-C then sends
.B SIGINT
to that group. When no program runs, Ctrl-C ends a built-in command (such
as
.BR sleep )
or drops the typed line, and the shell carries on.
.SS The prompt
.IB user @ host : directory
followed by
.B $
(or
.B #
for root). The host name is the first line of
.BR /etc/hostname ,
.B sieos
without one. When the terminal goes away (a closed window), the shell ends.
.SS Built-in commands
.BR cat (1),
.BR cd (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR chown (1),
.BR clear (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR echo (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR help (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR logout (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR mem (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR sleep (1),
.BR snap (1),
.BR stat (1),
.BR sync (1),
.BR tag (1),
.BR touch (1),
.BR uptime (1),
.BR battery (1),
.BR whoami (1),
and
.BR poweroff (8)
(with
.B pwd rmdir tags untag id groups exit reboot suspend
on the same pages).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
A login shell: print
.BR /etc/motd ,
then start in the user's home directory (or
.B /
if it cannot be entered).
.TP
.BI \-c " line"
Run the words after
.B \-c
as one line, then end with its exit status. No exit status message is
printed and programs stay in the shell's own process group (Ctrl-C reaches
them through the job the shell belongs to).
.BR make (1)
runs its recipes so.
.SH EXIT STATUS
A command's status: 0 for success. 127: the program was not found
.RB ( "unknown command" );
126: it could not be started; 128 plus the signal's number when a signal
ended it (130 for Ctrl-C). At the prompt a non-zero status is printed as
.BI "name: exit status " n\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/motd
shown by a login shell
.TP
.B /etc/hostname
the host name of the prompt
.TP
.B /etc/users
the user's name and home
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
echo "two  spaces" > note.txt   # the quotes keep both
sh \-c "ls /bin"
fetch \-o page.html http://example.com &
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR help (1),
.BR login (8),
.BR make (1)
.SH BUGS
No pipes, no redirection other than that of
.BR echo (1),
no variables and no scripts: one line at a time.
